====================================================================================
THE PATTERN, CHECKED: when can a group NORMALISE the degenerate locus away?
====================================================================================

 CLAIM.  A degenerate locus D can be normalised away by a group G iff G acts with D as the
 COMPLEMENT OF A FREE PART -- i.e. G moves every point of V \ D to a fixed normal form.  Then
 "is V contained in D?" becomes "is the normalised system INCONSISTENT?", which is a Groebner
 basis of <1> and is cheap and unambiguous.  Where G cannot do this, one is stuck with RADICAL
 MEMBERSHIP, which is expensive and easy to get wrong.

 INSTANCE 1 -- TNC (klein-S359).  D = {extreme coefficient vanishes}; G = the two
   scalings Lam -> mu Lam and u -> lam u.  G acts transitively on the extremes, so
   c_{-M} = c_N = 1 is reachable on V \ D.  Test becomes inconsistency:
     (M,N)=(1, 1): normalised Groebner basis = [1]  -> INCONSISTENT, TNC holds
     (M,N)=(2, 2): normalised Groebner basis = [1]  -> INCONSISTENT, TNC holds

 INSTANCE 2 -- the S357 system.  D = {alpha = beta = 0} is the ORIGIN.  Scaling fixes
   the origin, so NO group normalisation is available: the origin is always a solution,
   the ideal is never <1>, and one MUST test radical membership instead.
     basis = [a0, b0]   is it <1>? False
     -> NOT <1>, exactly as predicted: the origin is a genuine solution.  The correct
        test is whether the variety is ONLY the origin, i.e. radical membership --
        which is where I got the depth wrong in S357 (k <= 6 too shallow).

 SO THE PATTERN PREDICTS ITS OWN APPLICABILITY.  Scaling can normalise "some coordinate is
 nonzero" (a locus AWAY from the origin) but never "all coordinates vanish" (the origin
 itself, which every scaling fixes).  That is exactly why S359 could use inconsistency and
 S357 could not -- and it is checkable in advance, not after the fact.

